# Shell scripts must keep LF line endings in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Why this matters here: .githooks/pre-push is the client-side guard against pushing
# to main. If git checks it out with CRLF (the default on Windows when core.autocrlf
# is true), the shebang line becomes "#!/bin/sh\r" and a POSIX shell reports
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory". Git then treats the hook as failed to
# run, and on some paths simply skips it: a guard that looks installed but never
# blocks anything. Pinning eol=lf keeps the hook executable on Linux and macOS clones.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
